// recovery.nova — the pipeline behavior where panic recovery WILL live.
//
// ⚠️ THIS NO LONGER RECOVERS FROM ANYTHING — AND IT NEVER MEANINGFULLY DID.
//
// It used to be:
//     try { return next(); }
//     catch (err) { console.log("[RECOVERY] Caught exception: " + err); ...500... }
//
// `throw`/`try`/`catch` are GONE (specs §5.5, plan P2-12). Three reasons, all measured:
//
//  1. THE MESSAGE NEVER ARRIVED. `nova_throw` took a `long long`; the catch bound
//     `zext(setjmp_res)` — an i32. `di.nova`'s `throw "DI Error: ..." + key` reached this
//     handler as the integer `8472` — a string pointer truncated to 32 bits — and the line
//     above then re-stringified it as a NUMBER. Verified output:
//         [RECOVERY] Caught exception: 8472
//     The `err` this behavior logged could never be anything but a meaningless integer.
//
//  2. IT WAS UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR EXACTLY HERE. The implementation was setjmp/longjmp, and
//     this behavior runs inside an async HTTP handler — i.e. inside a C++20 coroutine.
//     `longjmp` out of a coroutine is UB. The one place this existed for is the one place
//     it was least defined.
//
//  3. IT LEAKED EVERY FRAME IT UNWOUND. With no real unwinding, ARC released only the
//     catching frame's try-block locals; every frame between the throw and here leaked
//     everything it owned.
//
// So this is now an honest pass-through: it does not pretend to catch. A real recovery
// barrier returns with the error model (`T | Error`, plan P2-13) — at which point `next()`
// returns `Response | HttpError` and this behavior maps the error case to a 500 by
// EXAMINING A VALUE: message intact, no unwinding, no UB under coroutines.
//
// Kept rather than deleted so registrations keep compiling, and so there is one obvious
// place for the barrier to reappear.
import web.request;
import web.response;
import web.status;
import web.mediator;

pub struct PanicRecoveryBehavior impl PipelineBehavior {
    // Signature MUST match the trait `PipelineBehavior.handle(self, req: any, next: () => any): any`
    // — the impl previously used concrete `request.Request`/`response.Response` types, a trait
    // signature mismatch that never compiled (no test imported this module). The pass-through
    // returns whatever `next()` yields.
    pub fn handle(self: PanicRecoveryBehavior, req: any, next: () => any): any {
        // Pass-through until `T | Error` lands (plan P2-13). Do not add a `try` here —
        // there is no `throw` to catch, by design.
        return next();
    }
}
